Function `find_preset` takes `key` as a read-only byte slice and returns the matching `Preset`, or the first preset (`amni_dark`) when not found.
Function `lerp_color` takes colors `a` and `b` and a 32-bit float `t`, clamps `t` between 0 and 1, linearly interpolates alpha, red, green, and blue channels independently, rounds each to the nearest byte, and returns the packed ARGB color.
Function `channel_lum` takes an 8-bit sRGB channel `c` as an unsigned 32-bit integer and returns its linear luminance as a 32-bit float: divide by 255; if at most 0.03928 return the quotient divided by 12.92, otherwise raise `(s + 0.055) / 1.055` to the power 2.4.
Function `luminance` takes color `col` and returns WCAG relative luminance `0.2126 * R + 0.7152 * G + 0.0722 * B` from the red, green, and blue bytes.
Function `contrast` takes colors `a` and `b` and returns `(hi + 0.05) / (lo + 0.05)` where `hi` and `lo` are the larger and smaller of their luminances.
Function `legible` takes foreground `fg`, background `bg`, and minimum ratio `min_ratio` as a 32-bit float. Return `fg` immediately if `contrast` is at least `min_ratio`; otherwise blend `fg` toward pure white if `luminance` of `bg` is below 0.25 or pure black otherwise across 16 steps until contrast reaches `min_ratio`, falling back to that pole color.
Function `build_palette` takes `key` as a read-only byte slice, `custom_accent` as an unsigned 32-bit color, and `high_contrast` as a boolean, returning a `Palette`. Look up `p` with `find_preset`; pick `custom_accent` when nonzero else `p.accent`.
For dark themes (`light` is false): `bg` is `p.bg`, `panel` blends `p.bg` 5.5 percent toward white, `panel2` blends 11 percent toward white, `line` blends 18 percent toward white, `line2` blends 28 percent toward white, `ink` is `0xFFF5F8FA`, `soft` is `legible` of `0xFFC5D0DC` on `panel` at ratio 7.0, `dim` is `legible` of `0xFF93A3B5` on `panel` at ratio 4.5, `acc` is `legible` of the chosen accent on `panel` at ratio 5.2, `gold` is `legible` of `0xFFFFC845` on `panel` at ratio 6.0, `err` is `legible` of `0xFFFF5C6C` on `panel` at ratio 5.2, and `ok` is `legible` of `0xFF2CE8A0` on `panel` at ratio 5.2.
For light themes (`light` is true): `bg` is `p.bg`, `panel` is white `0xFFFFFFFF`, `panel2` blends `p.bg` 6 percent toward black, `line` blends 16 percent toward black, `line2` blends 28 percent toward black, `ink` is `0xFF0A0E14`, `soft` is `legible` of `0xFF2B3544` on `panel` at ratio 7.0, `dim` is `legible` of `0xFF4A5568` on `panel` at ratio 4.8, `acc` is `legible` of the chosen accent on `panel` at ratio 5.0, `gold` is `legible` of `0xFF9E6400` on `panel` at ratio 5.0, `err` is `legible` of `0xFFC8233A` on `panel` at ratio 5.0, and `ok` is `legible` of `0xFF0B7A4B` on `panel` at ratio 5.0.
When `high_contrast` is true, force `ink` to white, `bg` to black, and `panel` to `0xFF0A0A0A` on dark themes (or `ink` to black, `bg` to white, and `panel` to `0xFFF2F2F2` on light themes) and re-run `legible` on `acc` against `panel` at ratio 7.0.
Function `car_type_scale` takes `screen_w`, `screen_h`, and `user_scale` as 32-bit floats and returns a `TypeScale` for glanceable, glare-proof car typography and touch targets. Compute `short_side` as the smaller of `screen_w` and `screen_h`, and `base` as `short_side / 540.0` clamped to 0.88 through 1.45 multiplied by `user_scale` clamped to 0.85 through 1.50. Set `telemetry_px` to `42.0 * base` clamped to 34 through 62, `title_px` to `22.0 * base` clamped to 18 through 32, `button_px` to `18.0 * base` clamped to 16 through 26, `body_px` to `16.0 * base` clamped to 15 through 22, `meta_px` to `14.0 * base` clamped to 13.5 through 19, `min_hit_px` to `62.0 * base` clamped to 56 through 88, `gutter_px` to `12.0 * base` clamped to 10 through 18, and `pad_px` to `14.0 * base` clamped to 10 through 22.
